 has to be evaluated, calibrated, and just fully optimized before liftoff.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.400
 Right.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.040
 You don't just wheel someone in and put a mask on them and start cutting.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.520
 No, not at all.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.720
 The surgical journey is strictly divided into three phases.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.800
 You've got preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.240
 And that first one, the preoperative phase is arguably where the groundwork for your survival

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.240
 is laid.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.280
 The assessment is just exhaustive.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.520
 If you've ever had surgery, you know the feeling, right?

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:22.880
 You're asked the same exact questions a dozen times by a dozen different people.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:23.880
 Yeah.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:24.880
 Like, what's your name?

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.280
 What are we fixing today?

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.680
 When did you last eat?

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.680
 Right.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.920
 It feels repetitive, maybe even a little annoying, but there is a massive physiological reason

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:33.920
 behind it.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.160
 It's not just checking a box.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.440
 No, it's not just about examining the spot where the surgeon is going to make an incision.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:45.080
 It's about anticipating exactly how the anesthesia combined with the physical trauma of the surgery

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.240
 itself is going to dysregulate your entire body.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.840
 I was looking at the national patient safety goals that drive this phase.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.480
 They have these huge checklists designed to prevent absolute nightmares.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:57.720
 Like wrong site surgery.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:58.720
 Right.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.520
 Where someone operates on the left knee instead of the right knee.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.080
 Or trying to prevent surgical site infections.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.960
 Or massive blood clots.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.400
 All critical things.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:11.760
 But one of the biggest hurdles before they even let you near an operating room is medication

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.760
 reconciliation.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.760
 Communication reconciliation is absolutely critical.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.560
 I mean, think about your blood.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:18.560
 Okay.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:24.320
 If you are taking aspirin or any kind of platelet inhibitor, your blood is medically designed

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.760
 not to clot efficiently.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:26.760
 Right.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:27.760
 It's thinner.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.880
 So now imagine a surgeon making a massive incision while your blood lacks that clotting

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.040
 capability.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.760
 That is a recipe for catastrophic uncontrollable hemorrhage.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.640
 So they have to stop it.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.680
 Protocol dictates that those medications typically need to be stopped seven to ten days before

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:43.680
 surgery.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.760
 The body just needs time to generate new, fully functioning platelets.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.240
 But it works the other way too, right?

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.360
 Like with beta blockers.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.360
 Precisely.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.080
 It's the total opposite issue.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.640
 If a patient is on beta blockers to manage their heart rate and blood pressure, stopping

