 them suddenly causes a rebound effect.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:01.920
 So the heart goes into overdrive.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.280
 The heart rate spikes, blood pressure, skyrockets, and the risk of a heart attack during surgery

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.960
 just goes through the roof.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:08.960
 Wow.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.500
 It's so critical that a patient takes their beta blocker within 24 hours of surgery that

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.760
 if they forget, the anesthesiologist has to immediately evaluate whether it's even safe

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:18.760
 to proceed.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:22.800
 So it's a massive biochemical balancing act before the scalpel even comes out.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:23.800
 Exactly.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.600
 And those baseline tolerances shift drastically depending on who the patient is, right?

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:28.600
 Oh, massively.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:33.160
 Like if you're listening to this and you happen to fall into the bariatric category, which

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:37.240
 I mean is a significant portion of the population considering obesity affects over 40 percent

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.240
 of US adults.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.240
 Yeah.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.240
 It's very common.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.440
 The preflight check changes entirely for that.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.680
 The physical realities of a higher body mass index introduce massive variables.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.560
 It's harder to get IV access for one, right?

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.920
 And wound healing is heavily delayed because adipose tissue or body fat just doesn't have

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.760
 a robust blood supply.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:00.600
 But the single biggest red flag for a bariatric patient during that pre-op assessment is their

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:01.920
 airway.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.080
 Why the airway?

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:07.680
 Because there is a remarkably high prevalence of obstructive sleep apnea or OSA in this

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:08.680
 population.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:11.960
 See, I always thought of sleep apnea as just a snoring issue.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.640
 Why is it a red flag for surgery?

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.360
 Because of how anesthesia actually works.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:20.280
 Sleep apnea means the tissues in the back of the throat naturally tend to collapse and

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.240
 block the airway when the muscles relax during normal sleep.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:24.240
 Okay.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.640
 Now introduce general anesthesia.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.520
 Anastasia doesn't just put you to sleep.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.320
 It medically paralyzes you.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:31.320
 Oh, wow.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.920
 It forces total profound muscle relaxation.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:39.540
 So if a patient has undiagnosed sleep apnea, the moment those drugs hit their system, their

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:40.880
 airway doesn't just narrow.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:42.720
 It completely collapses.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.280
 And they can't breathe at all.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.480
 The anesthesiologist suddenly has a literal life or death fight on their hands to force

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.720
 a breathing tube past that collapsed tissue before the patient's oxygen levels just plummet.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:58.320
 So catching that risk in the pre-op interview changes the entire intubation strategy?

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.640
 Exactly.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.160
 You're mapping the structural integrity of the airway beforehand.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:07.480
 And you see a totally different set of structural vulnerabilities when you look at gerontologic

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:08.480
 considerations.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:09.480
 Right.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:10.480
 Like older adults.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:11.480
 Oh, absolutely.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:16.080
 If you're over 65, your body simply doesn't handle metabolic stress the way it did at 30.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.600
 That's a great way to put it.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.040
 Older adults generally have much lower cardiac reserves, meaning their heart can't pump as

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:23.040
 hard.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.040
 Right.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.960
 Their heart simply can't ramp up its pumping capacity to meet the sudden massive stress

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:28.440
 of a surgical trauma.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.680
 Furthermore, their kidneys and their liver, which are the organs responsible for filtering

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:36.800
 and breaking down medications, are functionally depressed.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.480
 So does that mean the anesthesia stays in their system longer?

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:41.480
 Exactly.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:47.160
 A dose of a drug that a 30-year-old would metabolize and wake up from in, say, two hours might

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.680
 keep an 80-year-old deeply sedated for six hours.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.240
 That is a huge difference.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.880
 They are incredibly sensitive to these chemical agents.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.120
 And then there's also the thermal issue.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:59.120
 Like getting cold.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:00.120
 Yeah.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.800
 Most have decreased subcutaneous fat.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:06.320
 They don't have the insulation to maintain their body heat, especially since they are

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:07.800
 wearing a paper thin gown.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.840
 They're fasting, and they're about to be wheeled into an operating room that is kept

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:13.720
 incredibly cold.

