 Okay.

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:41.400
 So it's outside.

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:46.120
 A spinal anesthesia goes deeper directly into the subarachnoid space, mixing with the cerebral

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:48.000
 spinal fluid itself.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:51.440
 Which carries its own unique risks, like the dreaded spinal headache.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:52.840
 Yes.

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:56.680
 If the puncture allows cerebral spinal fluid to leak out after the needle has removed the

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:58.640
 pressure around the brain drops.

00:26:58.640 --> 00:26:59.640
 Ouch.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:03.320
 When the patient sits up, gravity pulls the brain downward slightly, stretching the meninges

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.280
 and causing a severe pounding post-anesthetic headache.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:11.560
 But the risk that really caught my eye with local anesthetics, like just the lidocaine the

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:15.240
 injectinum in area, is something called last.

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.440
 Local anesthetic systemic toxicity.

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:18.440
 Yes.

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:19.440
 Last.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:21.800
 That is a critical safety issue.

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:25.840
 Local anesthetics are meant to stay localized in the tissue, but if the provider is injecting

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:31.000
 near a bundle of nerves and the tip of the needle inadvertently punctures a blood vessel.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:32.760
 It goes right into the bloodstream.

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:33.760
 Exactly.

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:39.280
 That numbing agent is injected directly into the systemic blood circulation and travels

00:27:39.280 --> 00:27:41.040
 straight to the brain and the heart.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:43.360
 And the brain doesn't like being numbed.

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:44.360
 Not at all.

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:48.040
 The patient might suddenly report a metallic taste in their mouth or ringing in their ears

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:49.680
 or numbness around their lips.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:50.880
 Just from a local shot.

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:51.880
 Yes.

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:56.360
 And then if the provider doesn't stop injecting immediately, those auditory changes rapidly

00:27:56.360 --> 00:28:01.920
 escalate to massive generalized seizures, followed by cardiovascular collapse and respiratory

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:02.920
 arrest.

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:07.360
 The heart literally forgets how to beat because the electrical pathways are blocked by the

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:08.360
 anesthetic.

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:10.200
 The fix for that is fascinating.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:15.160
 The text says the treatment is airway management and administering intravenous lipid emulsions.

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:16.160
 Yes.

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:17.160
 Lipid emulsions.

00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:19.680
 They literally inject purified fat into your veins.

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:20.680
 Why?

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:23.880
 Because local anesthetics are highly lipid soluble.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:24.880
 They love fat.

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:25.880
 Oh, I see.

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:30.400
 By injecting a lipid emulsion into the bloodstream, you create a lipid sink, the toxic anesthetic

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:35.440
 molecules detach from the heart and brain tissue and bind to the fat molecules circulating

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:36.440
 in the blood.

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:37.440
 So just soak that up.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:38.440
 Exactly.

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:41.000
 Effectively neutralizing the toxicity until the body can clear it out naturally.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:43.240
 That is brilliant biochemistry.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:47.160
 So these are the massive hidden dangers of the intraoperative phase.

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:50.600
 The surgical team isn't just dealing with the physical trauma of the surgery itself.

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:51.600
 No, not at all.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:56.200
 They are simultaneously managing the profound physiological disruptions caused by the drugs

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:58.360
 making the surgery possible.

00:28:58.360 --> 00:29:00.400
 But eventually the scalpel goes down.

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:01.760
 The incisions are closed.

00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:03.240
 The anesthesia is turned off.

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:06.480
 Which brings us to the third phase of the perioperative journey.

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:07.720
 The fragile awakening.

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:08.720
 Right.

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:10.840
 Surviving the OR is only half the battle.

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:15.080
 Now the patient is rolled out of the surgical suite and into the post anesthesia care unit

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:16.080
 or the PACU.

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:17.840
 This is a critical transition.

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:19.000
 Very critical.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:23.640
 The PACU is almost always located directly adjacent to the operating rooms and there

00:29:23.640 --> 00:29:25.000
 is a good reason for that.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:26.560
 Because they're still basically under.

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:31.480
 The patient is emerging from that controlled induced coma but the drugs don't just instantly

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:32.680
 vanish.

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:36.720
 Their protective reflexes like the ability to swallow or cough haven't fully returned

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:37.720
 yet.

00:29:37.720 --> 00:29:38.720
 So they are super vulnerable.

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:42.760
 They are in an incredibly fragile state and if something goes wrong, the surgeons and

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:45.280
 anesthesiologists need to be mere seconds away.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:49.000
 The primary battle for a PACU nurse is the airway.

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:50.000
 Always the airway.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:54.360
 They are laser focused on preventing hypoxemia which is dangerous low oxygen levels in the

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:55.520
 blood.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:59.280
 And hypercapnia which is a buildup of carbon dioxide.

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:03.560
 And the most immediate mechanical threat to breathing isn't lung failure.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:04.960
 It's the patient's own tongue.

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:05.960
 Yes.

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:07.520
 Hypofaryngeal obstruction.

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:09.960
 Which is such a clinical term for something so basic.

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:11.200
 It is.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:15.360
 When you are deeply sedated, the muscles of your neck and fairing sloughs all their tone.

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:17.160
 They become flaccid.

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.360
 Because the patient is lying flat on their back, gravity just takes over.

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:24.200
 The lower jaw drops back and the heavy base of the tongue falls directly against the back

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:25.600
 of the throat.

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:27.320
 Physically sealing off the airway.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:30.800
 It's exactly what happens when someone snores loudly, right?

00:30:30.800 --> 00:30:31.800
 Exactly.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:36.320
 But vastly more dangerous because the patient cannot wake themselves up to fix it.

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.080
 So what does the nurse do?

00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:41.880
 If you are listening to this and you're picturing a high tech medical intervention, it's actually

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:44.960
 incredibly low tech and deeply physical.

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:48.000
 The nurse is right by the bedside watching the rise and fall of the chest.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:52.620
 If they see the chest moving but don't hear air escaping or if they hear a harsh choking

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:54.760
 sound, they step in immediately.

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:55.760
 What's the move?

00:30:55.760 --> 00:31:00.880
 The physical intervention is the head tilt chin lift or the jaw thrust maneuver.

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:04.480
 The nurse places their fingers behind the angle of the patient's jaw and physically pulls

00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:06.400
 the jawbone forward.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:08.320
 So pulling the jaw pulls the tongue.

00:31:08.320 --> 00:31:09.320
 Right.

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:12.440
 Because the tongue is attached to the jaw, this manually lifts the tongue off the back

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:14.800
 of the throat, popping the airway open.

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:18.580
 And the nurse will literally hover their hand over the patient's nose and mouth just to

00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:21.600
 feel the warm exhale of carbon dioxide.

00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:24.760
 It is deeply intimate hands on nursing care.

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:25.760
 Absolutely.

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:30.400
 Once the airway is secure, the next major hurdle in the pack you is post-operative nausea

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:32.840
 involving or PONV.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:37.220
 We touched on this earlier regarding anxiety but it is a massive issue.

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:39.880
 Up to 30% of surgical patients experience it.

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:42.520
 And it's not just that throwing up is unpleasant, right?

00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:44.800
 In a pack you setting, throwing up can kill you.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:45.800
 Yes.

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:48.680
 Because again, those protective reflexes aren't fully online.

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:52.720
 If a semi sedated patient vomits, they cannot reliably protect their airway.

00:31:52.720 --> 00:31:53.720
 They can aspirate.

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:55.280
 Meaning they inhale the vomit.

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:56.280
 Right.

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:57.760
 Down their trachea and into their lungs.

00:31:57.760 --> 00:32:00.000
 Gastric fluid is highly acidic.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:05.160
 If stomach acid hits the delicate tissue of the lungs, it causes severe bronchial spasms,

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:08.880
 instantaneous chemical pneumonitis and massive pulmonary edema.

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:10.920
 That sounds horrific.

00:32:10.920 --> 00:32:13.400
 The lungs literally burn and fill with fluid.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:18.000
 Which is why the pack you nurses are aggressive about treating nausea, the absolute second a

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:20.160
 patient complains of feeling queasy.

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:21.720
 They don't wait for the vomiting to start.

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:22.880
 Never.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:26.640
 And they have a whole arsenal of medications that attack the nausea from completely different

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:27.840
 physiological angles.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:33.520
 They do, they use gastrointestinal stimulants like meadow clopramide, which works by increasing

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:34.760
 gastric emptying.

00:32:34.760 --> 00:32:39.400
 Basically, it forces a stomach to squeeze its contents down into the intestines.

00:32:39.400 --> 00:32:42.200
 So there's simply nothing left in the stomach to throw up.

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:43.200
 Exactly.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:47.600
 They use phenathisines like permethazine for severe cases, which target the vomiting center

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:48.600
 in the brain.

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:53.360
 But the gold standard drug you see constantly is on to citron, also known as zofran.

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:54.360
 Right.

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:55.360
 Zofran.

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:59.240
 Therapeutic serotonin receptors in the brain and gut, preventing the nausea cascade with

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:01.080
 very few side effects.

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:02.560
 And it's not just drugs.

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:06.440
 The source has actually highlighted non-pharmacologic interventions for nausea that are gaining

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:07.440
 traction.

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:08.600
 Yeah, those are interesting.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:13.280
 Like using specific aromatherapy essential oils, or placing an alcohol swab under the

00:33:13.280 --> 00:33:14.960
 patient's nose.

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:19.760
 The sharp scent disrupts the nausea signals in the brain, it's fascinating how multifaceted

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:20.760
 the approaches.

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:25.160
 Well, once the patient's vital signs are stable, their airway is clear, and their pain and

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:26.600
 nausea are controlled.

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:31.600
 They are discharged from the PACU and transferred to the regular clinical surgical unit.

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:37.360
 And this is where the focus shifts from immediate survival to monitoring for longer term post-operative

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:38.360
 complications.

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:39.360
 Right.

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:43.000
 And there is one complication mentioned in the texts that honestly reads like something

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:44.400
 out of a horror movie.

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:46.320
 I'm talking about wound complications.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:48.000
 Yeah, these are severe.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:50.760
 If you've ever had stitches, you worry about them popping.

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:56.720
 But the clinical distinction between wound dehesense and wound evisceration is profound.

00:33:56.720 --> 00:33:59.640
 These are major structural failures of the surgical site.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:03.080
 They usually happen between day five and day eight after surgery.

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:04.080
 What triggers it?

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:09.360
 Often, a sudden immense pressure in the abdomen, like a severe bout of coughing, sneezing,

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:11.080
 or forceful vomiting.

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:13.160
 So what is dehesense?

00:34:13.160 --> 00:34:16.520
 Dehesense is the disruption or separation of the surgical incision.

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:21.520
 The sutures or staples give way and the layers of skin or underlying muscle separate the wound

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:22.520
 splits open.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:23.520
 Ouch.

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:28.160
 It is a very serious failure of the closure and it requires careful medical management

00:34:28.160 --> 00:34:29.880
 to prevent infection.

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:33.560
 But the internal organs remain contained inside the body cavity.

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:35.840
 Okay, an evisceration?

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:38.920
 Evisceration is a catastrophic medical emergency.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:43.920
 It is the actual protrusion of internal organs, most commonly the loops of the bowel bursting

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:48.240
 out through the separated incision and spilling outside the abdominal cavity.

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:49.240
 That is just...

00:34:49.240 --> 00:34:50.240
 Wow.

00:34:50.240 --> 00:34:51.240
 Wow.

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:56.760
 The text note that patients often describe feeling a sudden giving way sensation or a sudden

00:34:56.760 --> 00:35:02.920
 gush of sero-sanguine is fluid that pinkish watery blood soaking through their bandages.

00:35:02.920 --> 00:35:04.960
 Yeah, that's the warning sign.

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:07.320
 Imagine being the nurse walking into that room.

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:08.520
 What is the immediate response?

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:13.520
 Because obviously, instinct tells you to push the organs back in, but you absolutely cannot

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:14.520
 do that.

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:16.280
 You never push the organs back in.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:17.280
 Never.

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:21.240
 Pushing them risks tearing the fragile bowel or introducing massive infection.

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:24.560
 The nurse's immediate response is pure crisis management.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:28.480
 They instantly call for help and place the patient in a low Fowler position.

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:31.080
 Which is lying slightly back with a knees bent up.

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:32.080
 Right.

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:35.960
 This specific position relaxes the abdominal muscles and reduces the tension on the open

00:35:35.960 --> 00:35:39.320
 wound preventing more organs from spilling out.

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:41.240
 And then they have to protect the exposed bowel.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:42.240
 Yes.

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:46.720
 The nurse covers the protruding organs with sterile dressings soaked in sterile saline.

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:49.520
 You have to keep the bowel totally moist.

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:51.080
 Because of it dries out.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:56.200
 If the delicate serosa layer of the bowel dries out in the room air, the tissue will die and

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:57.960
 become necrotic.

00:35:57.960 --> 00:36:01.920
 The surgeon is notified immediately and the patient is rushed straight back down to the

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.640
 operating room for an emergency closure.

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:10.360
 It perfectly illustrates why post-operative nursing requires such intense vigilance.

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:13.080
 It's not just handing out ice chips and fluffing pillows.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:14.480
 Far from it.

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:18.680
 But let's talk about the more common daily battle on the surgical floor.

00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:21.600
 Pain management and getting the patient moving.

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:26.080
 For decades the standard protocol was just to hit the patient with heavy IV opioids like

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:28.320
 morphine or fentanyl until they were numb.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:29.320
 True.

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:33.600
 But we now know that heavy opioid use is actually terrible for recovery.

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:34.600
 Really?

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:35.600
 Yes.

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:39.280
 Opioids cause massive respiratory depression preventing the patient from taking the deep

00:36:39.280 --> 00:36:42.080
 breaths necessary to keep their lungs clear.

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:46.760
 They paralyzed the gastrointestinal tract leading to agonizing constipation and bowel

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:47.760
 obstructions.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:48.760
 Oh, that makes sense.

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:52.520
 They essentially slow the entire body down right when it needs to be waking up.

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:57.840
 Which is why there has been a massive shift toward ERAS, enhanced recovery after surgery.

00:36:57.840 --> 00:36:58.840
 ERAS, yes.

00:36:58.840 --> 00:37:01.240
 The entire philosophy is changing.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:06.480
 ERAS aims to get the patient independent and walking as fast as humanly possible.

00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:10.720
 And they achieve that pain controlled through multimodal analgesia.

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:12.440
 Multimodal is the key word here.

00:37:12.440 --> 00:37:16.320
 It means attacking the pain pathways from multiple angles simultaneously.

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:19.520
 So you don't have to rely on massive doses of any single drug.

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:21.720
 So layering the pain meds.

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:26.760
 Instead of just a morphine drip, an ERAS protocol might use a combination of a regional nerve

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:30.960
 block placed by the anesthesiologist before the surgery even begins.

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:36.400
 Then postoperatively, they give scheduled around-the-clock doses of intravenous acetaminophen.

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:37.720
 All in an IV.

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:38.720
 Exactly.

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:43.360
 They add non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs or NSAIDs to attack the inflammation at

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:44.880
 the tissue level.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:49.680
 They might even use neuropathic pain modulators like Gabapentin to quiet the nerve signals.

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:54.880
 And if they do need opioids, they often use patient controlled analgesia or a PCA pump.

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:56.920
 I love the psychology of the PCA pump.

00:37:56.920 --> 00:37:57.920
 It's a great tool.

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:01.120
 It's a machine connected to the IV with a little button the patient holds.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:05.920
 And when they feel pain, they push the button and it delivers a small preset dose of medication.

00:38:05.920 --> 00:38:08.920
 It is brilliant for several reasons.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:10.340
 First it eliminates the delay.

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:13.920
 The patient doesn't have to ring a call bell, wait for the nurse, wait for the nurse to

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:16.840
 pull the medication, and wait for it to be administered.

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:18.320
 They just push it and get it.

00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:21.160
 Second, it keeps the drug level stable in the blood.

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:25.040
 Instead of a massive spike in pain followed by a massive dose of drugs that knocks them

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:29.600
 out, they get tiny, frequent doses that keep the pain at a manageable hum.

00:38:29.600 --> 00:38:30.600
 That's way better.

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:34.800
 But perhaps most importantly, it gives the patient a psychological sense of control over

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:39.560
 their own body, which directly lowers that anxiety we talked about earlier.

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:43.880
 And the end goal of all that perfectly managed pain control is early ambulation.

00:38:43.880 --> 00:38:47.440
 Getting the patient out of bed and walking down the hallway, sometimes just hours after

00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:49.440
 major surgery.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.720
 Because lying in a hospital bed is genuinely one of the most dangerous things you can do

00:38:52.720 --> 00:38:54.200
 after a trauma.

00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:55.200
 Exactly.

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:57.880
 Bed rest is the enemy of recovery.

00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:02.760
 Early ambulation forces the patient to take deep breaths, expanding the alveoli in the lungs

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:05.160
 and preventing adelectasis and pneumonia.

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:06.520
 I guess the lungs working.

00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:10.800
 It physically stimulates the gastrointestinal tract, encouraging the bowels to wake up and

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:13.480
 reducing painful abdominal distension.

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:18.200
 And most critically, it keeps the blood pumping vigorously through the deep veins in the legs,

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:22.520
 which is the body's primary natural defense against venous thromboembolism.

00:39:22.520 --> 00:39:24.520
 Deadly blood clots.

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:29.640
 The entire post-operative care plan is a frantic race to get the patient's body systems functioning

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:31.440
 independently again.

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:32.640
 It really is.

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:38.120
 So if we look back at where we started, we've fully mapped the orchestrated crisis.

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:42.320
 The planning, the execution, and the recovery of a scheduled surgery.

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:47.840
 It's a massive deployment of resources, personnel and technology for a highly predictable physical

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:48.840
 trauma.

00:39:48.840 --> 00:39:49.840
 Right.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:53.600
 But what happens when the trauma is entirely unexpected?

00:39:53.600 --> 00:39:55.520
 When there is no pre-flight check.

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:59.640
 When the patient's baseline is a complete mystery and the clock is already ticking.

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:01.840
 That's a whole different world.

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:06.680
 We are going to leave the beautifully controlled sterile environment of the OR and step through

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:10.600
 the chaotic sliding front doors of the emergency department.

00:40:10.600 --> 00:40:13.520
 It is a completely different universe.

00:40:13.520 --> 00:40:16.720
 Emergency nursing operates on a fundamentally different paradigm.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:21.440
 The environment is fast-paced, highly technology-driven, and inherently unpredictable.

00:40:21.440 --> 00:40:22.440
 Your chaos.

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:27.120
 The central guiding principle of emergency care is rapid assessment, treatment, and referral.

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:31.040
 You have absolutely no idea what is coming through those doors in the next five seconds.

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:34.680
 But when it does, the care must be rendered immediately without hesitation.

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:38.520
