00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:42.360
 I've always thought of ER triage like a bouncer at a crowded nightclub.

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:45.040
 But it's a complete inversion of how a normal line works.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:46.040
 How so?

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:49.000
 A normal bouncer looks for the VIPs and lets them skip the line.

00:40:49.000 --> 00:40:54.040
 An ER triage nurse is actively scanning the crowded waiting room, bypassing the loud demanding

00:40:54.040 --> 00:40:59.520
 people and looking for the quiet, pale person in the corner who is closest to collapsing

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:01.120
 and pulling them to the front of the line.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:02.120
 That's spot on.

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:06.560
 It completely overrides the polite societal rule of first-come-first-ser.

00:41:06.560 --> 00:41:08.240
 It's a perfect analogy.

00:41:08.240 --> 00:41:13.080
 The word triage actually comes from the French word "trier," which means "to sort."

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:14.880
 In the ER, nurses don't diagnose.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:16.040
 They sort.

00:41:16.040 --> 00:41:17.440
 They use a rating system, right?

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:18.440
 Yes.

00:41:18.440 --> 00:41:22.960
