00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:33.600
 Level 1 is the most urgent.

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:34.600
 Right.

00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:40.040
 Level 1 is a life-threatening emergency requiring continuous, all-hands-on-deck resuscitation.

00:41:40.040 --> 00:41:44.480
 Think cardiac arrest, a massive trauma, or profound shock.

00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:45.480
 And level 2.

00:41:45.480 --> 00:41:46.960
 Level 2 is emergent.

00:41:46.960 --> 00:41:51.200
 They might not be dying this exact second, but their condition could deteriorate rapidly,

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:54.040
 like severe chest pain or a stroke.

00:41:54.040 --> 00:41:58.720
 It scales all the way down to level 5, which is non-urgent, like a minor rash or a prescription

00:41:58.720 --> 00:41:59.720
 refill.

00:41:59.720 --> 00:42:01.560
 So they sort you fast.

00:42:01.560 --> 00:42:03.800
 Triage is a highly advanced clinical skill.

00:42:03.800 --> 00:42:08.080
 The nurse has mere minutes to collect vital signs, get a chief complaint, and make an

00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:12.720
 incredibly rapid clinical judgment to ensure the patients who need care the most do not

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:13.720
 wait.

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:18.120
 Once a critical patient is pulled from the waiting room or rolled in by EMS, the trauma

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:22.280
