00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:07.280
 Then we move to C.

00:43:07.280 --> 00:43:08.680
 Circulation.

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:11.440
 This is all about stopping the loss of blood and treating shock.

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:12.440
 Right.

00:43:12.440 --> 00:43:15.320
 External hemorrhage is usually obvious.

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:17.400
 Someone is bleeding heavily from a wound.

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:22.200
 The immediate response is direct, firm pressure over the artery, or applying a tourniquet high

00:43:22.200 --> 00:43:24.880
 and tight if direct pressure fails.

00:43:24.880 --> 00:43:27.520
 But the real tricky part of C is internal hemorrhage.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:29.560
 Yeah, that's what I was wondering about.

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:34.120
 The blood is escaping the vascular system, but it's hiding inside the anatomic spaces.

00:43:34.120 --> 00:43:35.680
 The abdomen, the pelvis, the chest.

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:38.200
 You can't see the blood leaving the body.

00:43:38.200 --> 00:43:42.840
 So how does an ER nurse standing over a patient spot a massive internal bleed?

00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:46.360
 They have to look for the systemic signs of hypovolemic shock.

00:43:46.360 --> 00:43:47.960
 They are looking for the alarms.

00:43:47.960 --> 00:43:51.680
 The cardiovascular system sets off when it realizes it is running dry.

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:52.680
 Like what?

00:43:52.680 --> 00:43:56.240
 Well, because there is less blood volume, blood pressure plummets.

00:43:56.240 --> 00:44:01.360
 To compensate and try to keep oxygen flowing to the brain, the heart rate spikes drastically,

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:02.960
 causing tachycardia.

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:07.560
 The patient will often be extremely thirsty and incredibly apprehensive or anxious as their

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:10.020
 brain senses the lack of oxygen.

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:13.080
 And there is a very specific physical sign on the skin, too.

00:44:13.080 --> 00:44:17.760
 Yes, cool, moist skin with delayed capillary refill.

00:44:17.760 --> 00:44:18.760
 Capillary refill.

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:20.200
 Yeah, the body is smart.

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:23.920
 When it's bleeding to death, it clamps down the blood vessels in the periphery, the arms,

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:28.800
 legs, and skin, shunting whatever blood is left to the core vital organs, the brain,

00:44:28.800 --> 00:44:30.320
 heart, and lungs.

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:32.000
 So the skin gets pale and cold.

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:33.000
 Right.

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:37.120
 If you press firmly on the patient's fingernail until it turns white and then let go, a healthy

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:40.560
