00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:42.480
 And if they're bleeding internally.

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:46.600
 In a patient with internal bleeding, that capillary refill is severely delayed.

00:44:46.600 --> 00:44:48.480
 It takes seconds for the color to return.

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:53.560
 The treatment for that internal volume loss is massive fluid resuscitation.

00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:59.800
 The team establishes two large bore IVs, usually 16 to 14 gauge, which are massive needles,

00:44:59.800 --> 00:45:05.720
 and they pump in isotonic saline solutions, or ideally massive transfusions of packed red

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:09.240
 blood cells to replace the oxygen carrying capacity.

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:11.920
 And then they rush them straight to surgery to find the leak.

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:14.400
 But bleeding is a visible physical trauma.

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:18.320
 The emergency department also handles invisible physiological traumas.

00:45:18.320 --> 00:45:19.320
 Think about poisonings.

00:45:19.320 --> 00:45:24.600
 And one of the most fascinating physiological battles involves carbon monoxide poisoning.

00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:25.600
 That's a brilliant example.

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:27.560
 I was hoping we'd get to this.

00:45:27.560 --> 00:45:30.400
 We all know carbon monoxide or CO is dangerous.

00:45:30.400 --> 00:45:32.400
 It's the silent killer.

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:37.360
 It happens from faulty home furnaces, industrial accidents, or tragic suicide attempts and

00:45:37.360 --> 00:45:38.880
 closed garages.

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:44.200
 But there is a detail in our sources that absolutely fascinated me regarding how it presents

00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:45.920
 clinically.

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:51.440
 The text explicitly warns nurses that skin color, specifically a pink cherry red or

00:45:51.440 --> 00:45:57.520
 even blue cyanotic skin tone, is not a reliable sign of carbon monoxide poisoning.

00:45:57.520 --> 00:45:58.520
 No, it's not.

00:45:58.520 --> 00:45:59.520
 Why is that?

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:03.360
 If you were suffocating from a lack of oxygen, shouldn't you turn blue?

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:04.360
 You would think so.

00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:09.480
 But to understand why you don't turn blue, you have to understand the chemistry of hemoglobin.

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:13.280
 Think of a red blood cell like a bus traveling through your bloodstream, and the hemoglobin

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:15.120
 molecules are the seats on the bus.

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:16.120
 Okay.

00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:17.120
 I like this analogy.

00:46:17.120 --> 00:46:21.440
 Normally, oxygen hops into a seat in the lungs, rides the bus down to your tissues,

00:46:21.440 --> 00:46:23.680
 hops off, and feeds the cell.

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:24.680
 Civil enough?

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:27.160
 But carbon monoxide is a bully.

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:33.040
 CO binds to the exact same seats on the hemoglobin bus, but it binds 200 times more aggressively

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:34.240
 than oxygen does.

00:46:34.240 --> 00:46:35.720
 200 times.

00:46:35.720 --> 00:46:36.720
 Yes.

00:46:36.720 --> 00:46:42.440
 When a CO molecule sits down, it forms a compound called carboxy hemoglobin, and it absolutely

00:46:42.440 --> 00:46:44.240
 refuses to get off the bus.

00:46:44.240 --> 00:46:48.500
 So the oxygen from your lungs is just standing on the curb, unable to board the red blood

00:46:48.500 --> 00:46:53.320
 cells, while the carbon monoxide rides endlessly through your circulatory system.

00:46:53.320 --> 00:46:58.160
 So the body's tissues are suffocating, but the blood itself looks totally fine.

00:46:58.160 --> 00:46:59.160
 Exactly.

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:00.760
 The brain's sensors are tricked.

00:47:00.760 --> 00:47:04.000
 They look at the blood and see that all the hemoglobin seats are full.

00:47:04.000 --> 00:47:07.440
 Because the seats are full, the blood remains bright arterial red.

00:47:07.440 --> 00:47:08.600
 Oh, wow.

00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:13.080
 Bright red blood circulating under the skin can actually make the patient look artificially

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:17.040
 healthy pink, or even a flushed cherry red.

00:47:17.040 --> 00:47:20.960
 They don't look cyanotic or blue because the hemoglobin isn't empty.

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:24.040
 It's just carrying poison instead of oxygen.

00:47:24.040 --> 00:47:27.160
 The patient just appears progressively intoxicated.

00:47:27.160 --> 00:47:31.920
 They develop a severe headache, confusion, extreme muscle weakness, and eventually slip

00:47:31.920 --> 00:47:35.160
 into a coma due to profound cerebral hypoxia.

00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:38.600
 Your brain is starving to death while swimming in bright red blood.

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:39.600
 It's terrifying.

00:47:39.600 --> 00:47:42.840
 And the scariest part is you can't just take them outside and give them a breath of fresh

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:43.840
 air.

00:47:43.840 --> 00:47:48.160
 If the CO refuses to give up its seat, what is the medical intervention?

00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:52.460
 You have to forcibly evict the carbon monoxide by completely overwhelming the system with

00:47:52.460 --> 00:47:53.560
 oxygen.

00:47:53.560 --> 00:47:59.200
 The immediate treatment is slamming the patient with 100% pure oxygen via a tightly fitting

00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:00.880
 non-rebreed their mask.

00:48:00.880 --> 00:48:02.240
 And if it's really bad?

00:48:02.240 --> 00:48:05.920
 In severe cases, they are placed in a hyperbaric oxygen chamber.

00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:10.840
 The extreme atmospheric pressure inside the chamber literally forces oxygen into the blood

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:15.920
 plasma, completely bypassing the hemoglobin bus, feeding the tissues directly and slowly

00:48:15.920 --> 00:48:20.040
 shortening the half-life of the carbon monoxide until it finally detaches.

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:22.360
 It's an incredible biological hijacking.

00:48:22.360 --> 00:48:23.360
 Truly.

00:48:23.360 --> 00:48:27.680
 The ER also acts as the catch-all for a bizarre array of environmental emergencies.

00:48:27.680 --> 00:48:33.680
 We've got heat stroke, severe frostbite, near drownings, but the section on bites caught

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:36.160
 my attention, specifically spider bites.

00:48:36.160 --> 00:48:39.120
 Yeah, spiders are a classic ER presentation.

00:48:39.120 --> 00:48:42.760
 The source is emphasized that there are only two significantly venomous spiders in the

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:46.400
 US, the brown ray clues and the black widow.

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:51.680
 And what is wild is that their venoms attack the body in completely polar opposite ways.

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:54.600
 They represent two totally different mechanisms of destruction.

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:56.680
 Let's look at the brown recluse first.

00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:59.400
 For brown recluse bites you might not even feel it.

00:48:59.400 --> 00:49:01.440
 It is typically painless at first.

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:08.080
 The systemic effects don't even begin to show up for 24 to 72 hours, but the venom is cytotoxic.

00:49:08.080 --> 00:49:10.720
 It literally digests cellular tissue.

00:49:10.720 --> 00:49:12.200
 It melts the skin.

00:49:12.200 --> 00:49:13.800
 Essentially, yes.

00:49:13.800 --> 00:49:19.640
 In about 10% of cases, the bite site turns purple, then black, leading to severe, expanding

00:49:19.640 --> 00:49:20.840
 tissue necrosis.

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:22.320
 Like rotting flesh.

00:49:22.320 --> 00:49:23.320
 Exactly.

00:49:23.320 --> 00:49:28.920
 Fish dies, forming a massive ulcer that can take months to heal and requires extensive,

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:31.500
 painful surgical wound care and abridement.

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:35.080
 I assume the black widow bite is a similar race against tissue damage?

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:37.560
 Actually, it's the exact opposite.

00:49:37.560 --> 00:49:39.880
 A black widow bite doesn't rot your skin.

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:41.920
 The venom is a potent neurotoxin.

00:49:41.920 --> 00:49:43.520
 It hijacks your nervous system.

00:49:43.520 --> 00:49:44.520
 Oh, wow.

00:49:44.520 --> 00:49:48.800
 The bite itself feels like a sharp pinprick, but within 30 minutes, the neurotoxin causes

00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:51.920
