00:51:14.480 --> 00:51:20.160
 So how does a nurse handle someone who is physically out of control in an already chaotic room?

00:51:20.160 --> 00:51:24.760
 For the overactive patient, the immediate goal is to gain control of the situation and

00:51:24.760 --> 00:51:28.280
 provide a grounding calming effect.

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:32.320
 The nurse's therapeutic approach is just as critical as any medication.

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:33.640
 How do they talk to them?

00:51:33.640 --> 00:51:39.360
 Medical protocol advises nurses to approach calmly, maintain a safe physical distance,

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:44.640
 introduce themselves clearly by name, and state very simply, "I am here to help you."

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:47.720
 They speak in short, single thought sentences.

00:51:47.720 --> 00:51:48.880
 Sit down here.

00:51:48.880 --> 00:51:49.880
 Take a breath.

00:51:49.880 --> 00:51:50.880
 So they don't overcomplicate it.

00:51:50.880 --> 00:51:55.760
 No, it is about aggressively deescalating the panic and establishing a tiny perimeter

00:51:55.760 --> 00:51:56.760
 of safety.

00:51:56.760 --> 00:51:59.400
 Conversely, you have the underactive patient.

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:03.600
 They might come in complaining of physical somatic issues like a stomach ache, profound

00:52:03.600 --> 00:52:08.640
