 them with specialized resources, like the VA National Center for PTSD.

00:53:39.920 --> 00:53:44.360
 They're triaging the mind just as rigorously as they triage a bleeding artery.

00:53:44.360 --> 00:53:45.360
 Exactly.

00:53:45.360 --> 00:53:50.400
 We spend a lot of time looking at how perfectly the hospital machinery works when it's one

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:53.920
 patient and a dedicated team of staff.

00:53:53.920 --> 00:53:58.520
 The ER manages these acute individual crises beautifully.

00:53:58.520 --> 00:54:03.320
 It's a finely tuned system of triage, stabilization, and referral.

00:54:03.320 --> 00:54:07.040
 But what happens when you flip that ratio?

00:54:07.040 --> 00:54:11.720
 What happens when the sliding glass doors blow open, and there are 50 critically injured

00:54:11.720 --> 00:54:14.840
 patients in the parking lot and only five nurses?

00:54:14.840 --> 00:54:16.720
 That's the nightmare scenario.

00:54:16.720 --> 00:54:22.400
 All those careful protocols, the ABCDE primary surveys, the one-on-one psychiatric de-escalation,

00:54:22.400 --> 00:54:24.160
 what happens to all of that?

00:54:24.160 --> 00:54:26.800
 It instantly becomes obsolete.

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:31.520
 When a community experiences a massive disaster, whether it's a natural event like a catastrophic

00:54:31.520 --> 00:54:37.040
 earthquake or a man-made horror like a terrorist bombing or a chemical spill, the local health

00:54:37.040 --> 00:54:40.680
 care system is instantly, completely overwhelmed.

00:54:40.680 --> 00:54:44.960
 It's defined as a mass casualty incident, or an MCI.

00:54:44.960 --> 00:54:50.040
 The defining characteristic of an MCI is an influx of patients that creates a severe,

00:54:50.040 --> 00:54:52.480
 paralyzing scarcity of medical resources.

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:58.560
 To even attempt to manage that kind of apocalyptic chaos, the response has to be militaristically

00:54:58.560 --> 00:55:00.120
 standardized.

00:55:00.120 --> 00:55:04.520
 The sources emphasize that disaster plans are just made up by individual hospitals, right?

00:55:04.520 --> 00:55:09.440
 No, they must adhere to the National Incident Management System, or NIMS, which is directed

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:11.720
 by the federal government through FEMA.

00:55:11.720 --> 00:55:16.680
 And at the local hospital level, they activate the hospital incident command system, or HICS.

00:55:16.680 --> 00:55:20.320
 HICS establishes a rigid top-down hierarchy.

00:55:20.320 --> 00:55:25.440
 You suddenly have an incident commander, operations chiefs, logistics chiefs.

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:30.440
 The goal is to rapidly organize the chaos, track the massive influx of patients, and

00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:33.040
 ruthlessly allocate the scarce resources.

00:55:33.040 --> 00:55:37.640
 But the most profound devastating shift that happens during a mass casualty incident is

00:55:37.640 --> 00:55:39.520
 the paradigm shift in triage.

00:55:39.520 --> 00:55:40.520
 This is huge.

00:55:40.520 --> 00:55:45.040
 We talked about the ESI system and the ER, where the bouncer pulls the most critically

00:55:45.040 --> 00:55:49.600
 ill, dying patient to the front of the line, and the team pours massive resources into

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:52.040
 saving that one life.

00:55:52.040 --> 00:55:56.160
 When a disaster, you have to completely abandon that philosophy.

00:55:56.160 --> 00:55:58.200
 Wait, abandon it entirely?

00:55:58.200 --> 00:56:02.600
 This is perhaps the hardest concept for medical professionals to adapt to.

00:56:02.600 --> 00:56:08.000
 In a disaster, the primary ethical goal shifts away from saving the individual.

00:56:08.000 --> 00:56:12.200
 It becomes a utilitarian calculation, doing the greatest good for the greatest number

00:56:12.200 --> 00:56:13.200
 of people.

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:14.840
 So it's pure math.

00:56:14.840 --> 00:56:17.320
 Decisions are no longer based on who is the sickest.

00:56:17.320 --> 00:56:21.200
 They are based strictly on the likelihood of survival, combined with the consumption

00:56:21.200 --> 00:56:22.200
 of resources.

00:56:22.200 --> 00:56:26.840
 And this brutal calculation is executed using a color-coded disaster tagging system.

00:56:26.840 --> 00:56:27.840
 Yes.

00:56:27.840 --> 00:56:32.600
 The nurses move through the field or the triage bay, rapidly sorting patients into four distinct

00:56:32.600 --> 00:56:33.600
 categories.

00:56:33.600 --> 00:56:35.600
 Green is minimal.

00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:37.040
 These are the walking wounded.

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:38.500
 They have minor burns or sprains.

00:56:38.500 --> 00:56:42.040
 They are told to sit in a designated area and wait.

00:56:42.040 --> 00:56:43.440
 Yellow is delayed.

00:56:43.440 --> 00:56:47.840
 Their entries are significant, maybe a stable fracture or a deep laceration, but they can

00:56:47.840 --> 00:56:52.240
 wait several hours without a direct threat to life or limb.

00:56:52.240 --> 00:56:53.440
 And you have red.

00:56:53.440 --> 00:56:54.440
 Red.

00:56:54.440 --> 00:56:55.440
 Red is immediate.

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:59.520
 These patients have light-threatening injuries, but crucially, those injuries are survivable

00:56:59.520 --> 00:57:02.320
 with rapid minimal intervention.

00:57:02.320 --> 00:57:06.760
 Think of a severe bleeding wound that can be stopped with a quick tourniquet or a collapsed

00:57:06.760 --> 00:57:09.520
 lung that can be relieved with a needle decompression.

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:12.080
 They get immediate transport to the treatment areas.

00:57:12.080 --> 00:57:14.040
 And then there's the black tag.

00:57:14.040 --> 00:57:15.040
 Expectant.

00:57:15.040 --> 00:57:16.600
 Yeah, this is the hardest one.

00:57:16.600 --> 00:57:21.480
 The text to find this is patients whose injuries are so extensive that their chances of survival

00:57:21.480 --> 00:57:26.040
 are unlikely, even if they received maximal definitive care.

00:57:26.040 --> 00:57:30.080
 We are talking about unresponsive patients with massive penetrating head trauma, high

00:57:30.080 --> 00:57:38.360
 spinal cord injuries, profound hypovolemic shock or massive 90% body surface burns.

00:57:38.360 --> 00:57:40.800
 They are separated from the other casualties.

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:45.040
 They are given comfort measures like pain medication if it is available, but they are

00:57:45.040 --> 00:57:47.800
 emphatically not given life-saving resources.

00:57:47.800 --> 00:57:49.960
 It is an agonizing reality.

00:57:49.960 --> 00:57:53.840
 I have to ask you about this, because from the outside, it sounds incredibly harsh.

00:57:53.840 --> 00:57:56.880
 I mean, imagine you are the nurse holding that clipboard.

00:57:56.880 --> 00:58:01.120
 You have to tie a black tag to someone who is still breathing, whose heart is still beating,

00:58:01.120 --> 00:58:04.480
 and just walk away to treat someone else who is less injured.

00:58:04.480 --> 00:58:05.720
 It's devastating.

00:58:05.720 --> 00:58:09.560
 How do medical professionals who have dedicated their entire lives and training to fighting

00:58:09.560 --> 00:58:13.560
 for every single heartbeat handle that ethical whiplash?

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:17.060
 They handle it with profound difficulty.

00:58:17.060 --> 00:58:22.080
 The text explicitly acknowledged the massive ethical conflicts and the deep moral distress

00:58:22.080 --> 00:58:23.840
 this causes.

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:28.120
 It conflicts with every instinct a nurse or doctor possesses.

00:58:28.120 --> 00:58:29.960
 I can't even imagine.

00:58:29.960 --> 00:58:33.040
 But you have to break down the brutal math of a disaster.

00:58:33.040 --> 00:58:36.440
 It is a severe disparity between needs and resources.

00:58:36.440 --> 00:58:41.240
 If a team spends 45 minutes, three nurses, and all their remaining blood products trying

00:58:41.240 --> 00:58:47.040
 to save one black tagged patient who is statistically likely to die anyway, then others die.

00:58:47.040 --> 00:58:48.040
 Exactly.

00:58:48.040 --> 00:58:52.520
 During those 45 minutes, six red-tagged patients who could have been saved with 10 minutes

00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:56.040
 of intervention each will bleed to death in the hallway.

00:58:56.040 --> 00:58:58.240
 The math demands the sacrifice.

00:58:58.240 --> 00:58:59.440
 Yes.

00:58:59.440 --> 00:59:04.240
 And this is exactly why rigid rapid assessment algorithms like start, simple triage, and

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:06.760
 rapid treatment are utilized.

00:59:06.760 --> 00:59:09.120
 How does start work?

00:59:09.120 --> 00:59:14.080
 Start is a flowchart based on three simple observations, respiration, perfusion, and

00:59:14.080 --> 00:59:15.340
 mental status.

00:59:15.340 --> 00:59:17.280
 Is there breathing rate over 30?

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:18.280
 Red-tag.

00:59:18.280 --> 00:59:20.560
 Is capillary refill longer than two seconds?

00:59:20.560 --> 00:59:21.560
 Red-tag.

00:59:21.560 --> 00:59:22.880
 Can they follow simple commands?

00:59:22.880 --> 00:59:23.880
 No?

00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:24.880
 Red-tag.

00:59:24.880 --> 00:59:27.440
 So it's just a strict algorithm.

00:59:27.440 --> 00:59:30.680
 The protocol is designed to remove the human element.

00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:35.000
 It removes the emotional burden of choice in the chaotic moment to save the provider from

00:59:35.000 --> 00:59:37.520
 freezing and indecision.

00:59:37.520 --> 00:59:41.840
 But the psychological aftermath of making those decisions is immense.

00:59:41.840 --> 00:59:46.240
 The sources note that nurses and doctors are at a massive risk for compassion, fatigue,

00:59:46.240 --> 00:59:50.640
 severe PTSD, and depression after participating in disaster triage.

00:59:50.640 --> 00:59:52.920
 The trauma of the decisions haunts them.

00:59:52.920 --> 00:59:58.680
 This is why critical incident stress management, or CISM, debriefings are absolutely mandated

00:59:58.680 --> 01:00:04.160
 by hospitals to help the staff process the emotional wreckage of those impossible choices.

01:00:04.160 --> 01:00:08.140
 And the types of injuries they are sorting through in these MCIs are often catastrophic,

01:00:08.140 --> 01:00:14.160
 particularly when we start looking at terrorism and weapons of mass destruction or WMDs.

01:00:14.160 --> 01:00:19.520
 Let's dig into WMDs, starting with explosive devices and blast injuries.

01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:23.760
 When you see a bomb go off in a movie, the damage is usually depicted as flying shrapnel

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:26.720
 or a person being thrown physically against a wall.

01:00:26.720 --> 01:00:28.680
 Yeah, that's what Hollywood shows.

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:33.200
 But the reality is that the primary blast wave itself destroys the human body from the

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:34.600
 inside out.

01:00:34.600 --> 01:00:37.480
 The physics of an explosion are terrifying.

01:00:37.480 --> 01:00:43.880
 The primary blast wave is a massive, instantaneous, supersonic spike in atmospheric pressure.

01:00:43.880 --> 01:00:48.200
 As that invisible high pressure wave passes directly through the human body, it wreaks

01:00:48.200 --> 01:00:51.160
 havoc on any area that contains air.

01:00:51.160 --> 01:00:52.160
 Like the lungs.

01:00:52.160 --> 01:00:53.160
 Exactly.

01:00:53.160 --> 01:00:57.400
 The most common fatal primary blast injury is called blast lung.

01:00:57.400 --> 01:01:01.880
 As the pressure wave squeezes the air-filled lungs, it causes massive internal hemorrhage

01:01:01.880 --> 01:01:04.560
 and physically tears the delicate alveolar tissue.

01:01:04.560 --> 01:01:06.080
 That's horrifying.

01:01:06.080 --> 01:01:08.280
 Even worse, it causes air emboli.

01:01:08.280 --> 01:01:12.200
 The pressure forces tiny air bubbles directly into the bloodstream, which travel to the

01:01:12.200 --> 01:01:16.360
 brain or heart and cause immediate fatal strokes or heart attacks.

01:01:16.360 --> 01:01:21.280
 A patient with blast lung will present with severe dyspnea, hypoxia, and coughing up frothy

01:01:21.280 --> 01:01:22.280
 blood.

01:01:22.280 --> 01:01:24.920
 Another air-filled space that takes a massive hit is the ear.

01:01:24.920 --> 01:01:25.920
 Yes.

01:01:25.920 --> 01:01:29.760
 The tympanic membrane, the eardrum, is incredibly fragile.

01:01:29.760 --> 01:01:34.000
 A ruptured eardrum is actually the most frequent, reliable indicator that a patient was exposed

01:01:34.000 --> 01:01:36.360
 to a dangerous primary pressure wave.

01:01:36.360 --> 01:01:41.280
 Aside from explosives, disaster response teams drill rigorously for chemical and radiological

01:01:41.280 --> 01:01:42.680
 weapons.

01:01:42.680 --> 01:01:47.960
 With chemical weapons, the threat profile is defined by two crucial physical properties.

01:01:47.960 --> 01:01:48.960
 Volatility and persistence.

01:01:48.960 --> 01:01:50.960
 Those are key.

01:01:50.960 --> 01:01:55.640
 Volatility is the tendency for a chemical agent to become a vapor or a gas like phosgene

01:01:55.640 --> 01:01:57.240
 or cyanide.

01:01:57.240 --> 01:02:03.160
 Highly volatile agents are deadly when inhaled but dissipate relatively quickly.

01:02:03.160 --> 01:02:06.600
 Persistence is how long the chemical sticks around in the environment as an oily liquid

01:02:06.600 --> 01:02:08.320
 before evaporating.

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:12.000
 Highly persistent agents can contaminate an area for weeks.

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:16.520
 The sources categorize these chemical agents terrifyingly well based on what they do to

01:02:16.520 --> 01:02:17.520
 the body.

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:20.600
 You have nerve agents like Saren or VX.

01:02:20.600 --> 01:02:22.040
 What do they do?

01:02:22.040 --> 01:02:26.880
 These disrupt the mechanism that allows nerves to communicate with muscles, causing rapid,

01:02:26.880 --> 01:02:33.280
 massive neurological overstimulation, convulsions, pinpoint pupils, and respiratory failure.

01:02:33.280 --> 01:02:34.560
 And blood agents?

01:02:34.560 --> 01:02:39.240
 Like cyanide, which literally block your cells from using oxygen so your body asphyxiates

01:02:39.240 --> 01:02:41.360
 at the cellular level.

01:02:41.360 --> 01:02:45.920
 You have pulmonary agents which destroy the delicate membranes in your lungs, causing

01:02:45.920 --> 01:02:48.120
 you to drown in your own fluid.

01:02:48.120 --> 01:02:52.360
 And vesicants like mustard gas, which are blistering agents that cause severe chemical

01:02:52.360 --> 01:02:54.720
 burns on the skin and down the trachea.

01:02:54.720 --> 01:02:59.100
 The management of chemical exposures and a disaster scenario always starts with one

01:02:59.100 --> 01:03:02.080
 cardinal rule, limiting the exposure.

01:03:02.080 --> 01:03:03.080
 Decontamination.

01:03:03.080 --> 01:03:04.080
 Right.

01:03:04.080 --> 01:03:08.960
 Before a victim is ever allowed near a transport ambulance or the hospital doors, they must

01:03:08.960 --> 01:03:11.880
 undergo massive decontamination.

01:03:11.880 --> 01:03:16.720
 Rapid removal of all clothing, which removes 80% of the chemical, and thorough vigorous

01:03:16.720 --> 01:03:19.480
 washing with copious amounts of soap and water.

01:03:19.480 --> 01:03:23.520
 And the protective gear required for the medical teams handling these patients looks straight

01:03:23.520 --> 01:03:25.280
 out of a science fiction movie.

01:03:25.280 --> 01:03:26.440
 PPE levels, right?

01:03:26.440 --> 01:03:27.440
 Yes.

01:03:27.440 --> 01:03:31.280
 The EPA defines four levels of personal protective equipment.

01:03:31.280 --> 01:03:34.800
 Level A is the absolute highest, most extreme protection.

01:03:34.800 --> 01:03:39.880
 It is a fully encapsulating vapor-tight chemical resistance suit with a self-contained breathing

01:03:39.880 --> 01:03:42.000
 apparatus or SCBA.

01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:43.640
 So they are totally sealed in.

01:03:43.640 --> 01:03:47.720
 The provider is completely hermetically sealed off from the outside environment.

01:03:47.720 --> 01:03:52.000
 On the complete opposite end is level D, which is just your standard work uniform and a

01:03:52.000 --> 01:03:55.120
 basic mask, providing zero chemical protection.

01:03:55.120 --> 01:03:56.960
 What do hospitals use?

01:03:56.960 --> 01:04:01.400
 Most hospital first receiver teams use level C, which includes a splash resistant chemical

01:04:01.400 --> 01:04:04.520
 suit and an air purifying respirator mask.

01:04:04.520 --> 01:04:09.720
 Finally, in the grim realm of WMDs, we have to talk about radiation exposure.

01:04:09.720 --> 01:04:14.600
 Whether it's from a dirty bomb designed to disperse radioactive dust over a city or a catastrophic

01:04:14.600 --> 01:04:18.600
 nuclear event, the injury to the human body comes in three forms.

01:04:18.600 --> 01:04:23.480
 Thermal radiation, where the radiation wave passes through you, contamination, where radioactive

01:04:23.480 --> 01:04:28.280
 dust or liquid gets physically on your skin, an incorporation where you accidentally breathe

01:04:28.280 --> 01:04:33.440
 in or swallow the material and it is taken up directly into your cells and organs.

01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:38.620
 Defending against exposure is dictated by three remarkably simple rules--time, distance,

01:04:38.620 --> 01:04:39.620
 and shielding.

01:04:39.620 --> 01:04:44.960
 Minimize the time spent near the radioactive source, maximize your physical distance from

01:04:44.960 --> 01:04:50.560
 it, and put dense shielding, like lead or concrete, between you and it.

01:04:50.560 --> 01:04:55.240
 But if a patient is exposed to high doses of ionizing radiation, they develop a horrific

01:04:55.240 --> 01:04:59.200
 condition called acute radiation syndrome or ARS.

01:04:59.200 --> 01:05:03.240
 The mechanism of ARS is devastating to understand.

01:05:03.240 --> 01:05:07.320
 Radiation doesn't just burn skin, it rips apart DNA.

01:05:07.320 --> 01:05:12.800
 Specifically, it destroys cells that are actively in the process of dividing and reproducing.

01:05:12.800 --> 01:05:17.520
 So the systems in your body that rely on rapid, continuous cell reproduction are hit first

01:05:17.520 --> 01:05:19.440
 and destroyed the fastest.

01:05:19.440 --> 01:05:24.840
 This is exactly why cancer patients on chemotherapy lose their hair and get sick to their stomachs,

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:25.840
 right?

01:05:25.840 --> 01:05:28.280
 Chemotargets dividing cells.

01:05:28.280 --> 01:05:32.200
 Exactly the same principle, just via a massive environmental trauma.

01:05:32.200 --> 01:05:36.720
 The hematopoietic system, your bone marrow, which constantly churns out any blood cells

01:05:36.720 --> 01:05:41.040
 and immune cells, is the most sensitive and the first to completely fail.

01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:42.560
 So they lose their immune system?

01:05:42.560 --> 01:05:46.320
 This leads to massive immune collapse and uncontrollable internal bleeding because you

01:05:46.320 --> 01:05:48.280
 have no platelets.

01:05:48.280 --> 01:05:50.120
 Next is the gastrointestinal system.

01:05:50.120 --> 01:05:54.880
 The delicate lining of your stomach and intestines stops regenerating and simply sloughs off,

01:05:54.880 --> 01:05:57.480
 leading to bloody diarrhea and massive fluid loss.

01:05:57.480 --> 01:05:58.480
 In the brain?

01:05:58.480 --> 01:06:03.760
 At the most extreme, massive doses, the central nervous system itself fails, causing immediate

01:06:03.760 --> 01:06:06.080
 cardiovascular collapse and death.

01:06:06.080 --> 01:06:10.480
 The triage process for mass radiation exposure is chilling.

01:06:10.480 --> 01:06:14.600
 It categorizes victims by predicting their survival based almost entirely on how fast

01:06:14.600 --> 01:06:15.600
 their symptoms appear.

01:06:15.600 --> 01:06:16.600
 Right.

01:06:16.600 --> 01:06:17.600
 Time is the indicator.

01:06:17.600 --> 01:06:18.600
 Probable survivors.

01:06:18.600 --> 01:06:21.000
 So these are people who they have lower exposure.

01:06:21.000 --> 01:06:26.000
 They show some kind of minimal initial symptoms of maybe slight nausea.

01:06:26.000 --> 01:06:30.200
 Possible survivors show persistent symptoms of like vomiting within a few hours.

01:06:30.200 --> 01:06:33.360
 Their white blood cell counts drop steadily over days.

01:06:33.360 --> 01:06:36.240
 And then there are those who are improbable survivors.

01:06:36.240 --> 01:06:37.240
 Yeah.

01:06:37.240 --> 01:06:40.800
 So there are some situations that they've received a massive lethal dose.

01:06:40.800 --> 01:06:46.080
 And they have a cute, violent onset of, you know, severe vomiting, bloody diarrhea, neurological

01:06:46.080 --> 01:06:47.080
 confusion.

01:06:47.080 --> 01:06:49.240
 And it's literally within minutes of the exposure.

01:06:49.240 --> 01:06:52.320
 So in a disaster scenario, they are moved to the black tag category.

01:06:52.320 --> 01:06:56.920
 It is the ultimate stark reminder of the hard limits of medical science in the face

01:06:56.920 --> 01:07:00.160
 of overwhelming systemic trauma.

01:07:00.160 --> 01:07:05.480
 Which brings us to the final profound transition in our deep dive today.

01:07:05.480 --> 01:07:11.760
 There's the chaotic aftermath of a mass casualty disaster, the slow grinding progression of

01:07:11.760 --> 01:07:17.240
 a chronic disease, or simply the failure of a massive trauma intervention in the OR.

01:07:17.240 --> 01:07:20.720
 Eventually, the medical system reaches its absolute limit.

01:07:20.720 --> 01:07:21.720
 Yeah.

01:07:21.720 --> 01:07:22.720
 It always does eventually.

01:07:22.720 --> 01:07:27.120
 There comes a point where the focus must shift entirely from attempting to cure the

01:07:27.120 --> 01:07:30.280
 body to comforting the person inside it.

01:07:30.280 --> 01:07:33.680
 We are moving into palliative and end-of-life care.

01:07:33.680 --> 01:07:37.480
 It is a profound philosophical shift in the nature of care.

01:07:37.480 --> 01:07:41.440
 For their entire careers, doctors and nurses are trained to fight death.

01:07:41.440 --> 01:07:45.840
 Shifting gears to accept it requires a totally different framework.

01:07:45.840 --> 01:07:49.660
 And it's very important to understand that clinical distinction our sources make between

01:07:49.660 --> 01:07:53.080
 palliative care and hospice care.

01:07:53.080 --> 01:07:57.040
 The public often uses those terms interchangeably, and they are not the same thing.

01:07:57.040 --> 01:07:58.040
 No.

01:07:58.040 --> 01:07:59.040
 They are quite different.

01:07:59.040 --> 01:08:01.440
 Palliative care is the broader umbrella, right?

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:02.440
 Yes.

01:08:02.440 --> 01:08:08.560
 Palliative care uses a comprehensive interdisciplinary model, focusing entirely on aggressive symptom

01:08:08.560 --> 01:08:12.000
 management and psychosocial spiritual support.

01:08:12.000 --> 01:08:16.800
 Its goal is to drastically improve the quality of life for a patient facing a serious illness.

01:08:16.800 --> 01:08:18.720
 So they can still be getting treatment?

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:23.280
 The key distinction is that palliative care is appropriate for patients at any age and

01:08:23.280 --> 01:08:25.840
 at any stage of a serious illness.

01:08:25.840 --> 01:08:30.080
 A patient can absolutely receive palliative care to manage pain and nausea while they are

01:08:30.080 --> 01:08:35.120
 actively undergoing aggressive curative treatments like chemotherapy or radiation.

01:08:35.120 --> 01:08:40.560
 Hospice on the other hand is a very specific terminal subset of palliative care.

01:08:40.560 --> 01:08:45.680
 Hospice care focuses purely on comfort and dignity at the very end of life.

01:08:45.680 --> 01:08:49.240
 When a patient enters hospice, a massive decision has been made.

01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:54.080
 They have chosen to completely stop all curative disease modifying treatments.

01:08:54.080 --> 01:08:56.280
 The fight against the disease is over.

01:08:56.280 --> 01:09:01.360
 The medical focus shifts entirely to managing the symptoms of the dying process itself.

01:09:01.360 --> 01:09:05.200
 Tell patients and families understand and manage this massive transition.

01:09:05.200 --> 01:09:08.160
 The health care team maps out illness trajectories.

01:09:08.160 --> 01:09:09.920
 Like predicting how it will go.

01:09:09.920 --> 01:09:10.920
 Yes.

01:09:10.920 --> 01:09:14.840
 When you look at how human lives actually come to an end, it almost always follows one of

01:09:14.840 --> 01:09:17.480
 four distinct physical trajectories.

01:09:17.480 --> 01:09:21.560
 The first is sudden death, a massive heart attack or a car crash.

01:09:21.560 --> 01:09:23.360
 It's totally unexpected.

01:09:23.360 --> 01:09:28.120
 And interestingly, despite our fears, it actually accounts for the minority of all deaths.

01:09:28.120 --> 01:09:33.400
 The second trajectory is terminal illness, like an aggressive, untreatable cancer.

01:09:33.400 --> 01:09:38.680
 This looks like a relatively short, steady, predictable decline over a period of weeks

01:09:38.680 --> 01:09:40.600
 or months before death.

01:09:40.600 --> 01:09:42.880
 The third trajectory is organ failure.

01:09:42.880 --> 01:09:46.200
 Think of severe heart failure or end stage COPD.

01:09:46.200 --> 01:09:51.920
 This trajectory is a slow overall decline over years, but it's punctuated by severe,

01:09:51.920 --> 01:09:54.360
 terrifying episodic crashes.

01:09:54.360 --> 01:09:56.200
 Like any of them in the hospital over and over.

01:09:56.200 --> 01:09:57.200
 Right.

01:09:57.200 --> 01:09:58.880
 The patient suddenly can't breathe.

01:09:58.880 --> 01:10:00.200
 They end up in the ICU.

01:10:00.200 --> 01:10:04.460
 They recover slightly and go home, but their baseline is never as good as it was before

01:10:04.460 --> 01:10:05.460
 the crash.

01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:08.000
 They slowly staircase down.

01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:12.560
 And the fourth is frailty, often seen with advanced dementia or extreme old age, which

01:10:12.560 --> 01:10:17.840
 is a very slow, prolonged, dwindling decline that can stretch out for years.

01:10:17.840 --> 01:10:22.960
 Recognizing exactly which trajectory a patient is currently on allows the interprofessional

01:10:22.960 --> 01:10:28.600
 team, the physicians, the specialized hospice nurses, the social workers and the chaplains

01:10:28.600 --> 01:10:32.720
 to anticipate the physical symptoms and provide the right kind of support.

01:10:32.720 --> 01:10:37.160
 And what struck me in the texts is that a massive portion of this end of life evaluation

01:10:37.160 --> 01:10:38.960
 isn't physical at all.

01:10:38.960 --> 01:10:41.280
 It's deeply psychosocial and spiritual.

01:10:41.280 --> 01:10:42.280
 It has to be.

01:10:42.280 --> 01:10:44.960
 You are treating the whole human being.

01:10:44.960 --> 01:10:49.040
 The sources highlight the critical importance of a spiritual assessment offering a framework

01:10:49.040 --> 01:10:51.520
 called FICA as a guide, F-I-C-A.

01:10:51.520 --> 01:10:52.520
 FICA.

01:10:52.520 --> 01:10:53.520
 FICA.

01:10:53.520 --> 01:10:54.520
 Yeah.

01:10:54.520 --> 01:10:57.280
 The nurse sits with the patient and explores faith and belief.

01:10:57.280 --> 01:10:59.040
 What gives your life deep meaning?

01:10:59.040 --> 01:11:01.560
 They ask about importance and influence.

01:11:01.560 --> 01:11:04.880
 What role do your beliefs play and how you view your health?

01:11:04.880 --> 01:11:06.160
 They assess community.

01:11:06.160 --> 01:11:10.480
 Are you part of a spiritual support network, a church or a community group?

01:11:10.480 --> 01:11:12.760
 And finally, address and care.

01:11:12.760 --> 01:11:16.800
 How can our medical team address these deeply held beliefs in your daily care?

01:11:16.800 --> 01:11:20.360
 I found it really interesting how broadly the texts define spirituality.

01:11:20.360 --> 01:11:23.240
 It's not just asking, "What religion are you?"

01:11:23.240 --> 01:11:24.600
 No, not at all.

01:11:24.600 --> 01:11:29.720
 The domains of spirituality include finding meaning and purpose in life, seeking sources

01:11:29.720 --> 01:11:34.440
 of hope, and exploring the individual's attitude toward death.

01:11:34.440 --> 01:11:38.760
 It's explicitly noted that while things like financial stability cause immense stress,

01:11:38.760 --> 01:11:41.760
 they are not domains of spirituality.

01:11:41.760 --> 01:11:46.040
 Social care is about the nurse assessing the harmony or the painful discord between the

01:11:46.040 --> 01:11:51.200
 patient's deepest beliefs and the stark reality of their terminal diagnosis.

01:11:51.200 --> 01:11:55.880
 Because spiritual distress causes very real physical pain, doesn't it?

01:11:55.880 --> 01:11:56.880
 Oh, absolutely.

01:11:56.880 --> 01:12:00.800
 A patient who is terrified of being punished in the afterlife or who feels their life had

01:12:00.800 --> 01:12:05.640
 no meaning will experience drastically higher levels of physical pain that opioids simply

01:12:05.640 --> 01:12:06.800
 cannot touch.

01:12:06.800 --> 01:12:12.160
 Which brings up a massive reoccurring theme in this final section of our texts, hope.

01:12:12.160 --> 01:12:13.720
 Hope is huge.

01:12:13.720 --> 01:12:17.520
 I want to look at this because the text spend a tremendous amount of time discussing hope

01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:20.080
 and meaning in illness.

01:12:20.080 --> 01:12:23.600
 If I am a patient and a doctor walks in and tells me my cancerous spread, there is no

01:12:23.600 --> 01:12:28.320
 more treatment, and I am absolutely going to die in a matter of weeks.

01:12:28.320 --> 01:12:31.160
 How does a nurse foster hope in that room?

01:12:31.160 --> 01:12:35.440
 Doesn't the very concept of hope imply hoping for a cure?

01:12:35.440 --> 01:12:38.560
 That is a very common, very human misconception.

01:12:38.560 --> 01:12:43.240
 The clinical research highlighted in our sources shows that terminally ill patients demonstrate

01:12:43.240 --> 01:12:50.000
 an incredible, almost beautiful psychological resilience by repeatedly re-conceptualizing

01:12:50.000 --> 01:12:51.000
 hope.

01:12:51.000 --> 01:12:52.160
 Re-conceptualizing it.

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:53.160
 Yes.

01:12:53.160 --> 01:12:59.000
 Hope is not permanently chained to a cure, as the physical reality changes, hope shifts.

01:12:59.000 --> 01:13:04.000
 When a cure is definitively off the table, the human mind shifts hope to smaller, achievable

01:13:04.000 --> 01:13:05.000
 goals.

01:13:05.000 --> 01:13:10.560
 It changes from, I hope I beat this cancer and live another 20 years to something more

01:13:10.560 --> 01:13:15.120
 immediate like, I hope I am completely pain free today.

01:13:15.120 --> 01:13:19.200
 Or I hope I live long enough to see my daughter get married next week.

01:13:19.200 --> 01:13:20.200
 Exactly.

01:13:20.200 --> 01:13:25.840
 Hope provides meaning, direction, and optimism, even in the very shadow of death.

01:13:25.840 --> 01:13:30.920
 Hospice nurses are rigorously trained to engage in specific, hope-fostering activities.

01:13:30.920 --> 01:13:33.320
 These aren't medical interventions with a syringe.

01:13:33.320 --> 01:13:39.400
 No, they involve fostering the love of family and friends, encouraging spiritual reflection,

01:13:39.400 --> 01:13:43.720
 helping the patient maintain whatever tiny shred of independence they still have, like

01:13:43.720 --> 01:13:50.320
 just choosing what music to listen to, and constantly sharing humor and uplifting memories.

01:13:50.320 --> 01:13:55.920
 Conversely, they aggressively try to prevent hope hindering factors, like uncontrollable

01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:56.920
 pain.

01:13:56.920 --> 01:14:02.520
 Uncontrollable pain obliterates hope, but also feelings of abandonment by the medical team,

01:14:02.520 --> 01:14:07.000
 or the devaluation of the patient's personhood, treating them like a dying body rather than

01:14:07.000 --> 01:14:08.320
 a living person.

01:14:08.320 --> 01:14:13.400
 As the illness inexorably progresses, the focus shifts to managing the actual physical

01:14:13.400 --> 01:14:15.640
 stages of the dying process.

01:14:15.640 --> 01:14:19.440
 The sources break down the clinical symptoms on a timeline.

01:14:19.440 --> 01:14:24.800
 Months before death, weeks before, days before, and hours before.

01:14:24.800 --> 01:14:30.320
 Months before, you start to see a gradual, creeping weakness and a significant decrease

01:14:30.320 --> 01:14:31.600
 in appetite.

01:14:31.600 --> 01:14:34.080
 Minutes before, the systems start to falter.

01:14:34.080 --> 01:14:40.200
 You see neurologic changes like increased sleepiness or episodes of terminal delirium.

01:14:40.200 --> 01:14:45.480
 The cardiovascular system struggles, showing a rising, weak heart rate and a dropping blood

01:14:45.480 --> 01:14:46.480
 pressure.

01:14:46.480 --> 01:14:51.320
 When you get to the final days and hours before death, the symptoms escalate rapidly.

01:14:51.320 --> 01:14:56.240
 The kidneys shut down, resulting in anuria, a complete cessation of urine.

01:14:56.240 --> 01:14:59.080
 The skin loses its ability to regulate temperature.

01:14:59.080 --> 01:15:02.280
 It might become feverish or clammy and cold.

01:15:02.280 --> 01:15:07.240
 You see modeling of purplish, blotchy discoloration of the hands and feet as blood circulation

01:15:07.240 --> 01:15:09.720
 simply fails to reach the extremities.

01:15:09.720 --> 01:15:12.440
 And breathing patterns change drastically.

01:15:12.440 --> 01:15:17.360
 The patient might experience periods of apnea, where they stop breathing for 10 or 20 seconds

01:15:17.360 --> 01:15:22.240
 at a time, and there is development of what is often called the death rattle.

01:15:22.240 --> 01:15:27.080
 Right, which is caused by the patient's inability to clear natural secretions from their throat.

01:15:27.080 --> 01:15:33.000
 The nursing interventions in these final hours are pure, profound comfort, frequent repositioning,

01:15:33.000 --> 01:15:38.800
 gentle mouth care, and administering specific anti-colonergic medications to dry up those

01:15:38.800 --> 01:15:40.560
 throat secretions.

01:15:40.560 --> 01:15:44.960
 Not because the secretions are hurting the unconscious patient, but so the breathing sounds

01:15:44.960 --> 01:15:49.760
 less distressed and terrifying to the family sitting at the bedside.

01:15:49.760 --> 01:15:54.080
 There is one clinical phenomenon mentioned in the days before death timeframe that is

01:15:54.080 --> 01:15:56.720
 deeply fascinating and incredibly poignant.

01:15:56.720 --> 01:15:59.400
 Yes, the rally.

01:15:59.400 --> 01:16:01.800
 The techs call it a rally in energy.

01:16:01.800 --> 01:16:07.980
 A patient who has been deeply somulant, fading away, unable to eat or speak for days, might

01:16:07.980 --> 01:16:10.080
 suddenly wake up.

01:16:10.080 --> 01:16:13.760
 They experience a massive inexplicable surge in energy.

01:16:13.760 --> 01:16:18.200
 They might ask for a favorite meal, sit up in bed, and converse clearly and joyfully

01:16:18.200 --> 01:16:19.640
 with their family.

01:16:19.640 --> 01:16:23.000
 To anyone watching, it looks like a literal miracle recovery.

01:16:23.000 --> 01:16:27.440
 It's a well-documented medical phenomenon, but the techs warn that it is incredibly

01:16:27.440 --> 01:16:30.880
 heartbreaking if the family misinterprets it.

01:16:30.880 --> 01:16:33.920
 Imagine seeing your dying parents suddenly laughing and eating.

01:16:33.920 --> 01:16:37.960
 You would instantly think the doctors were wrong and a cure was happening.

01:16:37.960 --> 01:16:41.240
 Which is why the clinical guidance for the hospice nurse is so delicate.

01:16:41.240 --> 01:16:44.960
 The nurse must normalize the dying process for the family.

01:16:44.960 --> 01:16:49.360
 When a rally occurs, the nurse's intervention is to guide the family to lean in, take the

01:16:49.360 --> 01:16:53.900
 patient's lead, enjoy the conversation, eat the meal, take the photos.

01:16:53.900 --> 01:16:58.200
 But they must gently, compassionately, ensure the family understands that the surge of

01:16:58.200 --> 01:17:02.720
 energy is a known temporary part of the dying process.

01:17:02.720 --> 01:17:06.680
 It is a final gift, not a sign of remission.

01:17:06.680 --> 01:17:09.040
 Because soon after, the rally fades.

01:17:09.040 --> 01:17:11.600
 Death occurs and the patient's journey ends.

01:17:11.600 --> 01:17:15.360
 But the family's journey enters a new phase for reavement.

01:17:15.360 --> 01:17:21.640
 And the techs provide a really updated, fascinating view of how humans process grief and mourning.

01:17:21.640 --> 01:17:25.040
 We used to always hear about the Kubler-Ross five stages of grief.

01:17:25.040 --> 01:17:28.120
 Denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance.

01:17:28.120 --> 01:17:30.960
 It was taught almost like a rigid staircase you have to march up.

01:17:30.960 --> 01:17:31.960
 Right.

01:17:31.960 --> 01:17:37.360
 But modern psychology and newer nursing models acknowledge that grief is not a neat, linear

01:17:37.360 --> 01:17:38.360
 staircase.

01:17:38.360 --> 01:17:39.720
 It is messy.

01:17:39.720 --> 01:17:43.920
 The sources highlight the dual process model of coping with bereavement.

01:17:43.920 --> 01:17:45.160
 How does that work?

01:17:45.160 --> 01:17:49.720
 This model recognizes that grieving people actually oscillate continuously between two

01:17:49.720 --> 01:17:52.040
 completely different psychological states.

01:17:52.040 --> 01:17:54.920
 First, there is loss-oriented coping.

01:17:54.920 --> 01:17:56.360
 This is the traditional grief work.

01:17:56.360 --> 01:18:01.720
 It's crying, feeling the overwhelming intrusion of grief, dwelling on the bond with the deceased

01:18:01.720 --> 01:18:04.160
 and confronting the reality of the loss.

01:18:04.160 --> 01:18:05.800
 But they don't stay there.

01:18:05.800 --> 01:18:09.640
 They swing like a pendulum over to restoration-oriented coping.

01:18:09.640 --> 01:18:10.640
 Right.

01:18:10.640 --> 01:18:12.800
 This involves attending to necessary life changes.

01:18:12.800 --> 01:18:18.040
 Doing new things, temporarily distracting themselves from the grief, handling the finances and

01:18:18.040 --> 01:18:21.840
 taking on new roles and identities now that their loved one is permanently gone.

01:18:21.840 --> 01:18:26.240
 The brilliant insight of the dual process model is that this oscillation back and forth

01:18:26.240 --> 01:18:28.800
 is entirely healthy and normal.

01:18:28.800 --> 01:18:32.440
 You don't just achieve acceptance and stay there forever.

01:18:32.440 --> 01:18:33.880
 So you bounce between the two?

01:18:33.880 --> 01:18:37.800
 You might have a great restoration-oriented week where you feel totally fine and you

01:18:37.800 --> 01:18:39.560
 laugh and you go to work.

01:18:39.560 --> 01:18:43.400
 And then the next morning, you hear a song on the radio and you swing violently back

01:18:43.400 --> 01:18:46.960
 into profound loss-oriented weeping.

01:18:46.960 --> 01:18:50.880
 Normalizing this pendulum swing is a huge part of bereavement care.

01:18:50.880 --> 01:18:56.320
 It helps prevent families from feeling guilty or feeling like they are somehow grieving incorrectly

01:18:56.320 --> 01:18:57.680
 when they have a good day.

01:18:57.680 --> 01:19:03.120
 It's a profound, incredibly empathetic way to look at how human beings process the ultimate

01:19:03.120 --> 01:19:04.560
 loss of control.

01:19:04.560 --> 01:19:08.840
 And honestly, that brings this massive hour-long journey completely full circle.

01:19:08.840 --> 01:19:10.360
 Think about where we started.

01:19:10.360 --> 01:19:14.500
 We started in the operating room looking at the orchestrated crisis.

01:19:14.500 --> 01:19:19.840
 We saw how a massive, highly trained team uses checklists, surgical zones, precise chemical

01:19:19.840 --> 01:19:26.200
 anesthetics, and intense physiological monitoring to exert total, absolute control over a patient's

01:19:26.200 --> 01:19:27.400
 physical body.

01:19:27.400 --> 01:19:32.160
 Then we moved to the emergency department where that control is lost, and trauma teams use

01:19:32.160 --> 01:19:38.800
 rigid triage protocols and the ABCDE primary surveys to rapidly rest control back from

01:19:38.800 --> 01:19:41.240
 unexpected, chaotic trauma.

01:19:41.240 --> 01:19:46.960
 We scaled up to disasters and mass casualty incidents, where the sheer volume of human

01:19:46.960 --> 01:19:49.600
 suffering overwhelms the machinery.

01:19:49.600 --> 01:19:54.480
 The very philosophy of care shifts from saving the individual to rationing survival for the

01:19:54.480 --> 01:20:00.240
 greatest number, using brutal, color-coded tags to combat systemic overload.

01:20:00.240 --> 01:20:04.880
 And finally, we explored the absolute limits of all that medical intervention in palliative

01:20:04.880 --> 01:20:06.680
 and hospice care.

01:20:06.680 --> 01:20:11.600
 We saw what happens when the medical system stops trying to forcefully control the illness,

01:20:11.600 --> 01:20:16.840
 and instead marshals all its resources to support the patient and family in finding comfort,

01:20:16.840 --> 01:20:22.440
 dignity, meaning, and a reconceptualized hope in the final transition of dying.

01:20:22.440 --> 01:20:27.720
 Throughout this entire deep dive, we've seen how modern medicine absolutely relies on rigid

01:20:27.720 --> 01:20:34.120
 categorization to function, from ASA risk classifications before surgery, to the five

01:20:34.120 --> 01:20:39.880
 levels of the emergency severity index, to the written black tags in a disaster, all the

01:20:39.880 --> 01:20:43.920
 way to mapping the four trajectories of the dying process.

01:20:43.920 --> 01:20:48.480
 The massive machinery of the hospital system has to categorize us to operate.

01:20:48.480 --> 01:20:49.640
 It does.

01:20:49.640 --> 01:20:55.600
 But as we've seen at every single stage, medicine is ultimately a human endeavor, dealing with

01:20:55.600 --> 01:20:58.760
 incredible human unpredictability.

01:20:58.760 --> 01:21:03.640
 The categories, the tags, the checklist, they help us manage the chaos, but the actual patient

01:21:03.640 --> 01:21:09.880
 experience, your anxiety before surgery, your physiological resilience to a trauma, your

01:21:09.880 --> 01:21:16.160
 spiritual hope at the end of your life, and your family's grief always remains incredibly

01:21:16.160 --> 01:21:18.360
 stubbornly individual.

01:21:18.360 --> 01:21:22.240
 So as we wrap up, I want to leave you with a final thought to maul over.

01:21:22.240 --> 01:21:26.680
 We've just spent this deep dive breaking down the highly structured, almost rigid way the

01:21:26.680 --> 01:21:29.840
 medical system categorizes your physical vulnerability.

01:21:29.840 --> 01:21:30.840
 Right.

01:21:30.840 --> 01:21:37.160
 How does understanding the machinery of that system change how you view your own mortality?

01:21:37.160 --> 01:21:42.600
 And moving forward, how does it change your perspective on the flawed, stressed, but incredibly

01:21:42.600 --> 01:21:46.360
 human system you rely on to catch you when you fall?

01:21:46.360 --> 01:21:51.200
 It certainly makes you appreciate the delicate balance between the cold protocols written

01:21:51.200 --> 01:21:55.560
 on the clipboard and the warm empathy of the nurse who is holding it.

01:21:55.560 --> 01:21:56.800
 Absolutely.

01:21:56.800 --> 01:22:00.320
 Thank you for joining us on this intense, fascinating, deep dive.

01:22:00.320 --> 01:22:01.120
 We'll catch you on the next one.

